    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d211">

                <pb facs="0509" n="413" sameAs="frus:frus1879/pg413"/>

                <head>No. 190.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Evarts to Mr. Welsh</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1879-02-18"><hi rendition="#italic">February</hi> 18,
                        1879</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 233.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: At a recent convocation of boards of
                    railroad commissioners held at Columbus Ohio, a committee was appointed to
                    examine and report <pb facs="0510" n="414" xml:id="pg414"/> upon the methods of
                    taxation as respects railroads and railroad securities now in use in the various
                    States in the Union, as well as in foreign countries.</p>
                <p>Mr. Charles F. Adams, jr., of Boston, the chairman of that committee, has
                    requested the co-operation of this Department with a view to obtain the
                    information desired from abroad. As the object in view is deemed to be one of
                    national interest, I have to request you to obtain and transmit to the
                    Department, at your earliest convenience, such statements in detail as are
                    obtainable, and as may be necessay for a correct understanding of the systems of
                    railroad taxation in use in Great Britain. The information should especially
                    cover the following points, namely: <list>
                        <label>1st.</label>
                        <item>On what general recognized principle, if any, is the railroad taxation
                            of Great Britain based”?</item>
                        <label>2d.</label>
                        <item>In how far are the railroad companies taxed as holders of
                            realty?</item>
                        <label>3d.</label>
                        <item>In how far as holders of personalty?</item>
                        <label>4th.</label>
                        <item>In how far are they subject to a franchise tax?</item>
                        <label>5th.</label>
                        <item>In how far is their stock taxed as personalty to its owner?</item>
                        <label>6th.</label>
                        <item>What taxes are levied on the receipts of the companies, whether net or
                            gross?</item>
                    </list></p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>WM. M. EVARTS.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Note.</hi>—A similar instruction pent to the
                        minister to Austria-Hungary, Belgium, France, Germany, Italy, Russia, and
                        Switzerland.</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
